**🎯 Functional Correctness** | **🟡 Minor** | **⚡ Quick win**

**Apply ancestor opacity to ancestor backgrounds in the contrast audit.**

The loop composites each ancestor `backgroundColor` at its full declared alpha. It applies the accumulated `opacity` only to the text ink on line 42. CSS `opacity` also fades the element's own background and every descendant background. Consider an ancestor with `opacity: 0.5` and an opaque background. The browser paints that background mixed 50/50 with the surface behind it. The audit uses the unmixed color as the text background. The computed ratio can then differ from the painted ratio, so the audit can pass text that fails AA on screen. `docs/brand-review.md` says the audit covers "alpha and ancestor surfaces". The current loop does not meet that claim for faded containers.

Multiply the accumulated opacity into each background before blending. Update `opacity` first so that an element's own opacity applies to its own background.

<details>
<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>

```diff
--- "a/scripts/brand-browser-contrast.ts"
+++ "b/scripts/brand-browser-contrast.ts"
@@ -31,11 +31,13 @@
       for (let parent: HTMLElement | null = el; parent; parent = parent.parentElement) ancestors.unshift(parent);
       let background = [1, 1, 1];
       let opacity = 1;
       for (const ancestor of ancestors) {
         const css = getComputedStyle(ancestor);
-        background = blend(rgba(css.backgroundColor), background);
         opacity *= Number(css.opacity);
+        const fill = rgba(css.backgroundColor);
+        fill[3] *= opacity;
+        background = blend(fill, background);
       }
       if (!opacity) continue;
       const css = getComputedStyle(el);
       const ink = rgba(css.color);
```
</details>

